<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Eliot (diplomat)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1862–1931</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Charles Eliot</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Charles Eliot was a British diplomat, linguist and scholar who served as Commissioner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> from 1900 to 1904. He promoted European settlement in the Kenya highlands, setting aside land at the expense of the Maasai and other Africans, and resigned in 1904 after a dispute over Maasai land. He later served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and wrote on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-eliot-diplomat.jpg" alt="Charles Eliot (diplomat)" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bassano Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Charles_Norton_Edgcumbe_Eliot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
